{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DeYZFTnwzpTU7DCu8fKgsp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tSOURCE: Wii Fit\n2\t\n3\tGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n4\t\n5\tPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n6\t\n7\tGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n8\t\n9\tResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n10\t\n11\tPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-031600].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n12\t\n13\tTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge#make-a-game-with-my-3dge, in the engine's full workspace. The engine stays read-only, as the AGENTS.md it gives your game says: add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n14\t\n15\tArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n16\t\n17\tAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n18\t\n19\tAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n20\t\n21\tOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n22\t\n23\tWorking Environment and deliverable:\n24\tCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-031600] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-031600].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n25\t\n26\tPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n27\t\n28\tTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n29\t\n30\tEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n31\t\n32\t## This computer\n33\tChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n34\t\n35\t- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n36\t- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n37\t- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n38\t- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n39\t  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n40\t  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n41\t  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n42\t- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n43\t- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n44\t- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n45\t- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n46\t- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n47\t\n48\t### What this computer doesn't have\n49\t\n50\tNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n51\t\n52\t- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n53\t- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n54\t- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n55\t- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n56\t- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n57\t- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n58\t\n59\t### Sharing this computer\n60\t\n61\t- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n62\t- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n63\t- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n64\t- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n65\t\n66\t## Goal\n67\t\n68\tBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n69\t\n70\tScope\n71\t\n72\tCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n73\t\n74\tInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n75\t\n76\tAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n77\t\n78\tMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n79\t\n80\tNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n81\t\n82\t## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\n83\tDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n84\t\n85\tDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n86\t\n87\tTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n88\t\n89\t## Done\n90\tNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n91\t\n92\t## Source Information\n93\tCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n94\t- Date: 2026-11-05 03:16:00\n95\t- Source: the work it's based on\n96\t- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n97\t- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n98\t- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n99\t- Art Direction: style used\n100\t- Animation: system used\n101\t- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n102\t- Online: network features used, or none\n103\t- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n104\t- Research: research done, or none\n105\t- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n106\t- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n107\t- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n108\t\n109\tExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n110\t{\n111\t  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n112\t  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n113\t  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n114\t  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n115\t  \"aiHub\": {\n116\t    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n117\t    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n118\t    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n119\t    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n120\t    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n121\t    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n122\t    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n123\t    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n124\t    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n125\t    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n126\t  }\n127\t}\n128\tThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n129\t\n130\tTesting Feedback\n131\t\n132\t- How many issues did testing catch?\n133\t- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n134\t- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n135\t- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n136\t\n137\tEngine Feedback\n138\t\n139\t- What did you like most about the engine?\n140\t- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n141\t- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n142\t- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n143\t- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n144\t- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n145\t\n146\tPrompt Feedback\n147\t\n148\t- What did you like most about this prompt?\n149\t- What was confusing?\n150\t- What was the hardest thing to do?\n151\t- What would you change in this prompt?\n152\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e83c655a-6d0a-42b2-ac8e-79c6fd4e6b22","uuid":"bb157bdd-7a94-48f1-b83b-16e635162113","timestamp":"2026-11-05T10:16:07.918Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-031600__x54facc/work/TASK.md","content":"SOURCE: Wii Fit\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-031600].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge#make-a-game-with-my-3dge, in the engine's full workspace. The engine stays read-only, as the AGENTS.md it gives your game says: add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-031600] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-031600].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## This computer\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-05 03:16:00\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n","numLines":152,"startLine":1,"totalLines":152}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DeYZFTnwzpTU7DCu8fKgsp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjcB6XEewb9YqRcykfvQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RsHh8nsFEE12FiE3ABwBqj","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-031600__x54facc/work/PLAN.md","limit":400},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4168,"cache_read_input_tokens":19160,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4168},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e83c655a-6d0a-42b2-ac8e-79c6fd4e6b22","uuid":"ff592498-6a46-4e4e-95f0-2f9806b0be50","timestamp":"2026-11-05T10:16:08.219Z","request_id":"req_011CgjcB6HbtLqCj6hE2F5Kk","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01RsHh8nsFEE12FiE3ABwBqj":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-031600__x54facc/work/PLAN.md","limit":400}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RsHh8nsFEE12FiE3ABwBqj","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Game plan: a balance-and-wellness game inspired by Wii Fit\n2\t\n3\tYou build this without the director. This plan is complete: follow it, and where it is silent use your judgment and\n4\twrite the call in `labs/game/README.md`. Your own TASK.md still rules: it holds the file and folder names, the title\n5\trule, and the reports. Every path below is relative to your dev folder.\n6\t\n7\t**[tested]** marks what was actually run on this machine on 2026-11-04, against my-3dge at commit e7a1243. **[source]**\n8\tmarks what was read in that commit's code but not run. Trust both over your memory of three.js or the engine. Anything\n9\tunmarked is design: yours to tune.\n10\t\n11\tContents: 1 The game · 2 World and cast · 3 The one verb · 4 Controls · 5 Structure · 6 Activities · 7 The finale ·\n12\t8 Screens · 9 Multiplayer · 10 Art · 11 Animation · 12 Audio · 13 Writing · 14 Tech · 15 Engine traps · 16 Testing ·\n13\t17 Build order · 18 Done · 19 Reports · 20 Brief-to-plan map\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## 1. The game\n18\t\n19\t**Pitch.** You are a guest at the Little Fulcrum Wellness Retreat, a spotless island run by a retired bathroom scale\n20\twho stopped weighing people and now measures only how much you wobble. Every activity (poses, reps, step class,\n21\tpaddling, a slackline, a tilting sand garden, sitting perfectly still while a frog judges you) is played through one\n22\tverb: keep your centre of balance where it should be. Over five days the deadpan gets stranger. The plaques move.\n23\tMarbles roll by themselves. The staff keep asking you to stand further east. Little Fulcrum is balanced on a single\n24\trock. On the last night the storm comes, the island tips, and in the finale your thumb's balance *is* the island's.\n25\t\n26\t**Genre:** a 3D sports and wellness anthology of balance activities around a small walkable island, with a daily-ritual\n27\tmeta game and a comedy story. **Perspective:** third person throughout: a chase camera on the island, and a purpose-built\n28\tcamera for each activity (the table in section 6). **Hub genre:** Sports.\n29\t\n30\t**Pillars**, for every decision:\n31\t\n32\t1. **One verb, many bodies.** The left stick is your weight. A new activity changes what your weight moves, never how\n33\t   you move it. Fifteen seconds with any activity teaches the next one.\n34\t2. **Your thumb is the exercise.** The character's limbs follow the input directly, by scrubbing animation (section 11).\n35\t   Nothing plays \"a squat\" at you. You do the squat.\n36\t3. **Deadpan kindness.** Nobody shouts, nothing is a game over. The game is polite, slightly invasive, and proud of you\n37\t   in a way that worries you.\n38\t4. **The daily ritual.** A morning check, one big number, a stamp, a growing stack of pebbles. Coming back is the game.\n39\t5. **A small joke grows into the world.** The tilt starts as a sign that says \"Please keep to the centre\". It ends with\n40\t   a storm and a whole island on your thumb.\n41\t6. **A class, not a match.** Other people are on the next mat, wobbling. You can see them. It is enough.\n42\t\n43\t**Take from the source (feel and mechanics):** the centre-of-balance dot; holding still as a skill; lean-steered\n44\tminigames; breathing; rhythm stepping; rep counting; a daily test that yields an \"age\"; a talking fitness peripheral\n45\twith personal questions; a serene instructor; earning time-based currency in a plaza; passive-aggressive encouragement.\n46\t\n47\t**Never take (this is an original game, not a recreation):** the word \"Wii\" anywhere in the game except the About\n48\tscreen's Source line and the hub's source field; \"Fit Age\", \"Fit Credits\", \"Fit Piggy Bank\", \"Body Test\"; the source's\n49\ttrainer look; the Balance Board's shape or lines; Mii-like avatars; Wuhu Island; the source's minigame names, course\n50\tlayouts, menus, logo, fonts, jingles or melodies.\n51\t\n52\t**Never do, ever:** weight, BMI, calorie or body-size talk or numbers. All judgement is of *wobble*. That is also the\n53\tgame's best running joke: the scale refuses to weigh anyone.\n54\t\n55\t---\n56\t\n57\t## 2. World and cast\n58\t\n59\t**Little Fulcrum**: a green and sand island about 200 m across, one low summit, a clean white retreat scattered on it,\n60\topen sea to every horizon. It looks like a brochure. Everything is very slightly too tidy.\n61\t\n62\tStation layout, in metres (x east, z south, island centre at 0,0; tune by eye, keep the relations):\n63\t\n64\t| Place | Around | Holds |\n65\t|---|---|---|\n66\t| Ferry Pier and Beach | (0, 100) | Arrival, the ferry; the south shore |\n67\t| The Plaza | (0, 34), flat, y≈8 | Tare's Platform (Morning Check), Noticeboard, your Cairn |\n68\t| Pose Pavilion | (-52, 18), flat | Pose Class, Group Class: four mats, white floor, pale curtains, open sides |\n69\t| Still Pond | (-70, -12), a hollow | Still Pond |\n70\t| The Annex | (52, 22), flat | Reps Hall and Step Class under one long open roof, a mirror wall |\n71\t| Guest Huts | (64, 52) | Your hut: sleep ends the day |\n72\t| South-West Lagoon | (-60, 85) | The Line (headland to rock), Paddle Glide launch |\n73\t| Sand Terrace | (30, -48) | Sand Garden |\n74\t| North Cliff | (0, -88), 14 m drop | Cliff Plunge |\n75\t| Hoop Beach | (62, 84) | Hoop Patrol |\n76\t| Lighthouse | (84, -60) | Landmark; the storm's beam |\n77\t| East Terrace | (74, 8) | The \"high side\" in the finale |\n78\t| Centre Stone | (0, 0), the summit | A plain stone with a brass plate reading \"Do not\". The finale ends here |\n79\t\n80\t**Cast** (all original, pronouns as given):\n81\t\n82\t- **Tare** (it): the mascot and guide. A squat round dial bathroom scale on two stubby legs, face in the dial. Retired\n83\t  from weighing. Hops beside you. Chirpy, nosy, secretly sad. Keeps the secret of the rock badly.\n84\t- **Instructor Plumb** (they): head instructor. Tall, narrow, utterly vertical. Pale mint tracksuit, eyes always closed\n85\t  in two calm arcs, one very long straight plait that has never once moved. Speaks slowly and flatly. In the storm the\n86\t  plait swings for the first time, and that is the most frightening moment in the game. At dawn Plumb opens their eyes,\n87\t  once.\n88\t- **Gerald** (he): a retired guest with immense calves, always already there, always finished. Says only \"Morning.\"\n89\t  until Day 4.\n90\t- **Bex** (they): a bored teenager sent here by a parent, holding a phone-shaped stone, annoyingly excellent.\n91\t- **Old Nan** (she): 91, Wobble Age 19. Stands where you left her. Nobody has seen her arrive.\n92\t- **The Gull** and **the Frog** (it): recurring menaces. The Gull lands on you at the worst moment. The Frog just looks.\n93\t- **You**: \"Guest\". Pick one of three looks and a tracksuit colour at the pier. No name entry.\n94\t\n95\t**The five-day story** (delivered in one-liners, never in walls of text):\n96\t\n97\t| Day | Beat |\n98\t|---|---|\n99\t| 1 Arrival | Ferry leaves. Tare greets you. First Wobble Age is terrible. Sign: \"Please keep to the centre.\" |\n100\t| 2 The Mat | Plaques have moved. \"Guests are reminded to distribute themselves evenly.\" |\n101\t| 3 The Water | Drinks slide along tables. A notice calls this \"a feature of the drink\". |\n102\t| 4 The Sand | Marbles in the Sand Garden roll with nobody there. Gerald speaks. At night Tare confesses: the island sits on one rock. \"We don't mention it to guests. You're a guest. I didn't mention it.\" |\n103\t| 5 The Tilt | Group Class. Then the storm, The Great Tilt (section 7), dawn, the last Morning Check, credits. |\n104\t| After | \"You may stay as long as you like. The ferry does not return.\" Free Play, and a fresh Morning Check every in-game day. |\n105\t\n106\t---\n107\t\n108\t## 3. The one verb: the Wobble Model\n109\t\n110\tThis is the game's core. Build it first, tune it in dev mode, and reuse it everywhere. It lives in the sim as a\n111\tcomponent `wobble` on each balancing body, one per seat.\n112\t\n113\t**The centre of balance (CoB)** is a point in a unit disc: `x` left to right, `y` back to forward. `r = 1` is the edge:\n114\tpast it you topple.\n115\t\n116\tEach step (1/60 s), for each axis:\n117\t\n118\t```\n119\ttarget  = slew(stick + mouse, 4 units/s) × (gentle ? 0.4 : 1) × reach      // clamped to the disc\n120\taccel   = gain × (target − cob) + instability × cob + noise(t) × (1 − calmEffect × calm) + disturbance\n121\tvel    += accel × dt;   vel *= exp(−damping × dt);   cob += vel × dt\n122\t```\n123\t\n124\t| Setting | Default | Range | What it does |\n125\t|---|---|---|---|\n126\t| `game.wobble.gain` | 14 /s² | 4–30 | How fast your weight follows your thumb |\n127\t| `game.wobble.instability` | 3 /s² | 0–10 | How hard you fall away from the centre |\n128\t| `game.wobble.damping` | 5 /s | 1–12 | How heavy the body feels |\n129\t| `game.wobble.noise` | 3.5 /s² | 0–8 | Sway strength |\n130\t| `game.wobble.noiseRate` | 0.15 Hz | 0.05–0.4 | Sway speed: a slow push you lean against |\n131\t| `game.wobble.reach` | 0.9 | 0.5–1 | Full stick's target; a held full stick topples, on purpose |\n132\t| `game.breath.period` | 8 s | 4–14 | One breath, half in, half out |\n133\t| `game.breath.calmEffect` | 0.5 | 0–1 | How much full calm quiets the sway |\n134\t\n135\tSway per axis, phases and small rate ratios `m` drawn once per seat from a named stream (`w.rng('sway')`), never\n136\t`Math.random`, and written with plain `Math.sin` so the sim's math swap reaches it:\n137\t\n138\t```\n139\tnoise(t) = ( sin(2π·rate·m1·t + p1) + 0.6·sin(2π·rate·1.7·m2·t + p2) + 0.3·sin(2π·rate·3.1·m3·t + p3) ) / 1.3      // mi in 0.8–1.25\n140\t```\n141\t\n142\t**These numbers are modelled, and the slow sway matters [tested in a small simulation of this exact model, 20 s, 10 seeds,\n143\tstaying in a ring of 0.22]:** no input 38%; a human-like thumb (250 ms reaction, proportional gain 1.2, a little jitter) 90%;\n144\ta sloppy thumb (350 ms, gain 0.8) 72%; a no-delay bot 100%. A ring of 0.16 drops the thumb to 65%. Off-centre seats out to\n145\t0.35 hold at 87%, and 0.6 starts to topple (so keep seats within 0.5). **At the 0.45 Hz sway first tried, doing nothing beat\n146\tthe human thumb,** because a 250 ms reaction turns correction into amplification, so keep the sway slow. Nobody can play by\n147\thand here: the delayed-thumb bot (section 16) is how you judge feel.\n148\t\n149\tThe universal 4 units/s slew is what makes keyboard feel analog: a quick tap is a small nudge, a held key is a full lean.\n150\tSticks barely notice it. Mouse adds its own target with a slow decay (section 4).\n151\t\n152\t**Topple.** `r > 1` for 6 steps: the body stumbles (`reactTo` flinch, a stumble clip, a creak), the activity decides the\n153\tprice, and the CoB returns to centre over a second. It is never a game over.\n154\t\n155\t**Breath.** The third input is both feet held. A guide ring swells and shrinks with `breath.period`. Holding both feet\n156\tfills `lungs`; letting go empties them. `sync` is a running match between your lungs and the guide. Good sync raises\n157\t`calm` (0–1), which quiets the sway, warms the picture slightly, softens the sound, and pays a bonus. Breath is used in\n158\tstill activities and is the charge mechanic in fast ones. Being out of sync has no penalty beyond losing the help.\n159\t\n160\t**Feet.** Left and right foot are the two triggers (analog) or shoulders. Used for steps, rhythm, paddling, jogging and\n161\tone-leg holds. Both together is breath, or a jump in Step Class. That is every input the activities need: **Lean, Left\n162\tFoot, Right Foot, Both Feet.**\n163\t\n164\t**Scoring.** Every activity yields 0–1000 and a rank: 0 **Present**, 300 **Adequate**, 550 **Composed**, 750 **Serene**,\n165\t900 **Unsettling**. Pebbles earned: 1 to 5 by rank.\n166\t\n167\t**No softlocks.** Every activity and every finale step ends by itself: each step has a time box, after which it moves on with\n168\ta remark (\"We will call that a start.\"). A player who puts the pad down reaches the results screen.\n169\t\n170\t**Balance targets the tests enforce** (section 16) on 10 seeds at default settings, in every activity: the perfect bot\n171\tscores ≥ 750, the human-like delayed thumb ≥ 550, the sloppy thumb ≥ 300, no input at all ≤ 500 and below the human-like\n172\tthumb. Random mashing never crashes.\n173\t\n174\t---\n175\t\n176\t## 4. Controls\n177\t\n178\tThe DualSense on a TV is the design target. Keyboard and mouse are native, not stand-ins.\n179\t\n180\t| Verb | DualSense | Keyboard | Mouse |\n181\t|---|---|---|---|\n182\t| Lean (activities) | Left stick (D-pad too) | WASD or arrows; Shift is gentle | Move: pointer-locked movement leans |\n183\t| Left foot / Right foot | L2 / R2 (L1 / R1 too) | Q / E | Left button / Right button |\n184\t| Both feet (breathe, jump) | L2 + R2 | Space | Both buttons |\n185\t| Pause | Options | Esc | |\n186\t| Walk (island) | Left stick | WASD; Shift walks slowly | |\n187\t| Look (island) | Right stick | | Move (pointer lock) |\n188\t| Talk, use, enter | Cross | E or Enter | Left button at the thing in view |\n189\t| Hop | Square | Space | |\n190\t| Programme (today's list) | Triangle | Tab | |\n191\t| Wave | Circle | C | |\n192\t| Menus | D-pad or stick, Cross, Circle | Arrows, Enter or Space, Esc | Hover moves focus, click picks |\n193\t\n194\tActivity-specific mouse feel: in Sand Garden the pointer's place on the tray is the tilt; in Hoop Patrol you circle the\n195\tmouse; Step Class is left and right click on the beat. On the island the thing at the centre of the screen gets a highlight\n196\tand its prompt, and left click uses it.\n197\t\n198\t**Prompts** follow the input used last (`input.lastDevice`), switching the same frame. Pad: PlayStation glyphs (Cross,\n199\tCircle, Square, Triangle, L1, R1, L2, R2, sticks) drawn as inline SVG. Settings has \"Button icons: PlayStation / Xbox\"\n200\t(A B X Y, LB RB LT RT). Keyboard and mouse: key caps and a mouse icon with the button lit. Never rely on the engine's\n201\t`input.prompt()` text for pads: it guesses the family from `Gamepad.id` and defaults to Xbox **[source]**.\n202\tRead the action's codes, pick the ones for the device with `deviceOf`, and map each code to your glyph.\n203\t\n204\t**Action maps** (`defineActionMap`, switched live with `engine.input.use(id)`):\n205\t\n206\t```ts\n207\t// actionMap:balance, every activity. [tested]: Space on both feet gives both held; values read move actions too.\n208\tdefineActionMap('actionMap:balance', {\n209\t  description: 'The lean, the two feet, and anything else, which still pond punishes.',\n210\t  actions: {\n211\t    forward: ['KeyW', 'ArrowUp', 'PadUp', 'PadLeftStickUp'],\n212\t    back: ['KeyS', 'ArrowDown', 'PadDown', 'PadLeftStickDown'],\n213\t    left: ['KeyA', 'ArrowLeft', 'PadLeft', 'PadLeftStickLeft'],\n214\t    right: ['KeyD', 'ArrowRight', 'PadRight', 'PadLeftStickRight'],\n215\t    footl: ['KeyQ', 'MouseLeft', 'PadLeftTrigger', 'PadLeftShoulder', 'Space'],\n216\t    footr: ['KeyE', 'MouseRight', 'PadRightTrigger', 'PadRightShoulder', 'Space'],\n217\t    gentle: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight'],\n218\t    act: ['KeyF', 'Enter', 'PadSouth', 'PadEast', 'PadWest', 'PadNorth'],\n219\t  },\n220\t  values: { 'game:r': 'right', 'game:l': 'left', 'game:f': 'forward', 'game:b': 'back', 'game:fl': 'footl', 'game:fr': 'footr' },\n221\t  lock: true,\n222\t});\n223\t```\n224\t\n225\tThe sim reads raw lean as `game:r − game:l`, `game:f − game:b` (a key absent is 0), not the camera-turned `move`.\n226\tTriggers count as \"held\" from 0.5 but their `values` are analog below that.\n227\t\n228\t`actionMap:roam` is the same walk block plus `jump: ['Space', 'PadWest']`, `use: ['KeyE', 'Enter', 'MouseLeft', 'PadSouth']`,\n229\t`walk: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight']`, `plan: ['Tab', 'PadNorth']`, `wave: ['KeyC', 'PadEast']`, `lock: true`.\n230\t\n231\tFront-end screens use the engine's system layer (`uiAccept`, `uiCancel`, `uiUp`…), which no map can rebind, with a map that\n232\thas `lock: false`, or a click on the canvas behind a menu captures the pointer **[source]**. Sand Garden uses a copy of the\n233\tbalance map with `lock: false`, so the pointer stays visible: the page sends its place as `game:pointer`.\n234\t\n235\tInside activities, read only the map's actions, never `uiAccept`: Space is also breath. Skipping a demonstration is `act`.\n236\tThe first time a mouse player needs the lean, show \"Click to lean with the mouse\": browsers grant pointer lock only in a click.\n237\t\n238\t**Mouse lean:** while pointer-locked in an activity, the page adds `pointermove`'s `movementX/Y` per frame and sends\n239\tthem with `engine.input.virtual.axis('game:mouse', [dx, dy])`. The sim keeps `mouseTarget += delta × sensitivity`,\n240\tdecaying with a 0.6 s time constant. Sensitivity lives in Settings.\n241\t\n242\t**Rumble:** a light `vibrationActuator.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on topple, on beats in Step Class, and growing with\n243\t`r` near the edge; try/catch around it; a Settings toggle.\n244\t\n245\t---\n246\t\n247\t## 5. Structure\n248\t\n249\t**The Programme.** A five-day residency: a first full play takes about 45 minutes. Each day is a Morning Check plus three\n250\tactivities, from a data table in `labs/game/scenes/data/days.ts`:\n251\t\n252\t| Day | Activities |\n253\t|---|---|\n254\t| 1 | Morning Check (tutorial) · Pose Class A (Seated Nothing, Patient Chair, Heron) · Still Pond · a walk to find the Noticeboard |\n255\t| 2 | Morning Check · Reps Hall · Sand Garden · Pose Class B (Archer, Comma, Napkin) |\n256\t| 3 | Morning Check · Paddle Glide · The Line · Step Class |\n257\t| 4 | Morning Check · Hoop Patrol · Cliff Plunge · Island Jog with Gerald |\n258\t| 5 | Morning Check · Group Class (you and CPU guests) · Pose Class C (Bridge, Lighthouse) · The Great Tilt |\n259\t| After | Final Morning Check, credits, then Free Play with every activity and daily checks |\n260\t\n261\tFinishing all of a day lets you sleep at your hut (walk there, or pick it from the Programme). Every activity can also be\n262\treplayed from the Noticeboard once met. Activities have no fail state: finishing at any rank counts.\n263\t\n264\t**Getting around.** You may walk everywhere, and the island rewards it, but nothing requires it: every Programme and\n265\tNoticeboard item has \"Go\", which fades to the station. A controller player on a sofa never has to cross the island.\n266\t\n267\t**Wobble Age.** The Morning Check's result, from 18 to 82: `age = round(18 + 64 × (1 − s)^k)`, `k` is `game.age.k` (1.3).\n268\tOne big number, a remark, a stamp on the calendar, and a line graph of at most seven points. Always compared with yesterday,\n269\tin a tone of mild scientific surprise.\n270\t\n271\t**Pebbles and your Cairn.** Pebbles are the currency: 1 to 5 per activity by rank, plus 20 hidden around the island. You\n272\tnever spend them. They stack, one by one, on a physical cairn in the Plaza, with a satisfying clack. The cairn leans\n273\tslightly according to your last Wobble Age and stands true at 18. Thresholds unlock things, shown on the Noticeboard:\n274\t10 Expert variants (a smaller ring, more sway), 20 and 30 tracksuit colours and a headband, 40 the \"Unsettling\" stamp set.\n275\t\n276\t**Exploration.** 20 pebbles, 12 \"Did you know?\" plaques, and each cast member has 3–5 new lines each day. Sit on a bench\n277\tto watch the view with the Meditate clip. Wave at Gerald. Plaques visibly creep downhill across the days.\n278\t\n279\t**Tilt as story.** A value `island.tilt` starts at 0.4° on Day 1 and rises by 0.5° a day, so drinks slide, marbles roll,\n280\tloose props drift and the sea sits fractionally wrong at the horizon. Only the Great Tilt gives it weight in play.\n281\t\n282\t**Saves** go in the page store (`createStore`, prefix unique to your game): day, done list, ages, pebbles, finds,\n283\toutfits, bests, settings. The sim never reads them: the page hands the sim its starting state through a scene action\n284\t(`game.begin`, section 14). Settings has Erase with an in-game confirmation, never `confirm()`.\n285\t\n286\t**Built to grow.** Days, poses, charts, trays, courses, lines, lures and settings are all data tables, one file each, so\n287\tlater content is adding rows. Keep it that way: it is part of the brief.\n288\t\n289\t---\n290\t\n291\t## 6. Activities\n292\t\n293\tEvery activity is a module exposing the same shape: `{ id, title, station, camera, map, setup, step, teardown, score,\n294\tbot, attract, short? }` in `labs/game/scenes/events/`. `step` emits sim events; the page turns them into text, sound and\n295\tHUD. `bot` writes the same seat input a player does, and is used by tests, attract mode and dev auto-play. `short` is the\n296\t45-second form for rounds (section 9).\n297\t\n298\t| Activity | Station | Camera | Verbs |\n299\t|---|---|---|---|\n300\t| Morning Check | Plaza | Front, low, slow push-in | Lean, a foot |\n301\t| Pose Class | Pavilion | Three-quarter, drifting orbit round your mat | Lean, foot or both |\n302\t| Reps Hall | Annex | Side three-quarter, mirror behind | Lean scrubs the body |\n303\t| Step Class | Annex | Behind and above, class in view | Feet, both, some lean |\n304\t| Still Pond | Pond | Fixed portrait, front | Both, and nothing else |\n305\t| The Line | Lagoon | Behind and above, dolly along the line | Feet, lean x |\n306\t| Paddle Glide | Lagoon | Chase, low | Lean, feet |\n307\t| Sand Garden | Terrace | Top-down, slight perspective | Lean, or pointer |\n308\t| Cliff Plunge | North Cliff | Side dolly, falling follow, water-level splash | Both, lean |\n309\t| Hoop Patrol | Hoop Beach | Front, wide | Lean in circles, or feet |\n310\t| Island Jog | Whole coast | Chase (engine `cam3=chase`) | Stick, feet |\n311\t\n312\t### Morning Check (about 75 s)\n313\t\n314\tTare's Platform. Tare asks one yes/no question for the day (it changes only the remark). Then three tests:\n315\t\n316\t1. **Still Ring**, 12 s: stay inside a ring of r = 0.22, narrowing to 0.16 for the last 4 s. `A` = share of steps inside.\n317\t2. **Reach**, up to 24 s: six targets (r = 0.16) at radius 0.5 in a seeded order; hold each 0.5 s. `B` = 1 − time/24.\n318\t3. **One Foot**, 8 s: hold the foot Tare names; instability × 1.6; ring r = 0.3. Letting go pauses and drains. `C` = share inside.\n319\t\n320\t`s = 0.45A + 0.3B + 0.25C`; score `s × 1000`; Wobble Age as in section 5. Reveal: a large CSS dial spins past 100, \"recalibrating\",\n321\tovershoots, and settles. The Day 1 version introduces each verb with one line at a time.\n322\t\n323\t### Pose Class (about 2.5 min, three poses)\n324\t\n325\tPlumb demonstrates on the front mat (4 s, skippable). \"Now you.\"\n326\t\n327\t- **Enter:** a guide dot travels a curved path on the disc from centre to the pose's *seat*. Stay within 0.18 of it and\n328\t  progress advances; progress scrubs the pose strip, so the body goes into the pose exactly as your thumb does. If you never\n329\t  follow, Plumb waits 10 s and moves on: \"We will call that a start.\"\n330\t- **Hold:** `game.pose.hold` s (12) with the CoB in the seat ring (`game.pose.ring`, 0.2), breathing with the guide, while\n331\t  your off-seat lean visibly tips the body over. CPU guests hold too. Plumb does not move.\n332\t- **Release:** \"And rest.\" Score: `1000 × (0.7 × inRing + 0.3 × sync) − 60 × topples`, class mean is the result.\n333\t\n334\t| Pose (id) | What it is | Seat (x, y) | Rule |\n335\t|---|---|---|---|\n336\t| Seated Nothing (`nothing`) | Cross-legged, hands on knees | 0, 0 | Sway × 0.5; breath weighs 60% |\n337\t| The Patient Chair (`chair`) | Knees bent, arms forward | 0, −0.35 | Hold both feet throughout, so no breath; sway climbs with time |\n338\t| The Heron (`heron`) | One leg, foot on thigh, palms overhead | 0, 0 | Hold the standing foot; instability × 1.5 |\n339\t| The Archer (`archer`) | Long lunge, arms out | 0.35, 0.35 | Instability × 1.2 |\n340\t| The Sideways Comma (`comma`) | Side bend | 0.5, 0 then −0.5, 0 | Two holds, 8 s each |\n341\t| The Folded Napkin (`napkin`) | Forward fold | 0, 0.5 | Low camera; the Gull lands on your back halfway |\n342\t| The Polite Bridge (`bridge`) | On your back, hips lift | 0, 0 | Hips follow your lungs; breath weighs 70% |\n343\t| The Lighthouse (`lighthouse`) | Tiptoe, arms straight up | 0, 0 | Ring 0.14; sway × 1.2 |\n344\t\n345\t### Reps Hall (about 90 s, three exercises)\n346\t\n347\tThe signature \"thumb is the exercise\" activity. A tall gauge on the HUD shows a band moving at the tempo\n348\t(`game.reps.tempo`, 3 s a rep). Stick depth moves your marker and scrubs the body. Stay within 0.2 of the band to bank the\n349\trep. Gerald, beside you, does two for each of yours.\n350\t\n351\t- **Deep Knees** (10): pull back to squat, return to stand.\n352\t- **Polite Push-Ups** (8): hold both feet (\"palms on the floor\") and scrub the push-up clip with the stick.\n353\t- **Side Lunges** (10): stick x, alternating sides.\n354\t\n355\tThe band speeds up a little and sway rises near the end. Near the finish the counter cheats: \"9 of 10\" becomes \"10 of\n356\t11\". Plumb: \"Four more. I may be lying.\" Score: mean rep accuracy.\n357\t\n358\t### Step Class (about 100 s)\n359\t\n360\tRhythm on a step platform in the Annex, three phrases at 104, 112 and 120 BPM (`game.step.bpm` scales them), charts as\n361\tstrings in `labs/game/scenes/data/charts.ts`: `\"L . R . L . R . | L L R . B . . .\"`. Footprints scroll to a hit line on the\n362\tHUD, floor tiles light in the world. Notes: L, R, B (both, a jump) and side steps (lean plus foot).\n363\t\n364\tWindows: Perfect ±3 steps, Good ±7 (`game.step.perfect`, `game.step.good`). Combo adds up to × 1.5. The guests behind you\n365\tfollow your combo: lose it and they fall out of time, one by one, politely.\n366\t\n367\t**Beat audio goes on the effects bus, not the music bus.** Music defaults to 10%, so the song is only a bed. Sim `beat`\n368\tevents fire claps and kicks at full level. The bed has no drums, so nothing flams against it. Timing is in sim steps,\n369\tnever audio time.\n370\t\n371\t### Still Pond (75 s; 45 s in rounds)\n372\t\n373\tThe funniest thing in the game, and almost no input. You sit cross-legged on a jetty at dawn. **Composure** starts at 1000.\n374\tThe only correct input is breathing with the guide. Anything else is \"You moved\": stick past 0.25, any button, a stray\n375\tfoot, or mouse movement past a small threshold. Each costs `game.pond.penalty` (80), debounced 1 s, and sends a ripple\n376\tacross the pond while the Frog turns to look.\n377\t\n378\tScore: `Composure × (0.5 + 0.5 × sync)`, so doing nothing at all earns \"Adequate\".\n379\t\n380\tLures, from a seeded table, every 6–10 s, escalating:\n381\t\n382\t- A big glyph prompt for whichever device you use: \"PRESS NOW\".\n383\t- Tare: \"Psst. Is this on? Press ✕ for yes.\"\n384\t- The phone-shaped stone buzzes.\n385\t- The Frog sits on your knee. The Gull lands on your head.\n386\t- A fake pause menu slides in with \"Resume\" highlighted.\n387\t- \"Your controller appears to be disconnected. Press any button.\"\n388\t- Plumb, very quietly: \"You are doing it wrong.\"\n389\t- The credits roll. \"Thanks for playing! Press ✕ to continue.\"\n390\t\n391\tReal pause (Options, Esc) always works and costs nothing. Ending: \"You did nothing for seventy-five seconds. Nobody has\n392\tever been prouder of you. Nobody was watching.\"\n393\t\n394\t### The Line (about 75 s)\n395\t\n396\tA 40 m slackline across the lagoon with three flags. Alternate feet to step: each step moves 0.75 m and kicks you sideways\n397\t(`game.line.stepKick`, 0.35); the same foot twice is a stumble. Lean x fights it. Gusts every 6–10 s, telegraphed 1.5 s ahead\n398\tby ripples and a flag on Tare's little boat (`game.line.gust`). At 15 m the Gull lands on your arm, adding a steady bias\n399\tuntil you flick it off with a fast stick flip. Falling is a large splash, then Tare's boat takes you back to the last\n400\tflag: \"You have left the line. The line is fine.\" After 120 s the boat collects you: \"The line closes at this time.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e83c655a-6d0a-42b2-ac8e-79c6fd4e6b22","uuid":"4e82be33-ef71-47c4-a2d6-c131fb2bf4e2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T10:16:08.393Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-031600__x54facc/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Game plan: a balance-and-wellness game inspired by Wii Fit\n\nYou build this without the director. This plan is complete: follow it, and where it is silent use your judgment and\nwrite the call in `labs/game/README.md`. Your own TASK.md still rules: it holds the file and folder names, the title\nrule, and the reports. Every path below is relative to your dev folder.\n\n**[tested]** marks what was actually run on this machine on 2026-11-04, against my-3dge at commit e7a1243. **[source]**\nmarks what was read in that commit's code but not run. Trust both over your memory of three.js or the engine. Anything\nunmarked is design: yours to tune.\n\nContents: 1 The game · 2 World and cast · 3 The one verb · 4 Controls · 5 Structure · 6 Activities · 7 The finale ·\n8 Screens · 9 Multiplayer · 10 Art · 11 Animation · 12 Audio · 13 Writing · 14 Tech · 15 Engine traps · 16 Testing ·\n17 Build order · 18 Done · 19 Reports · 20 Brief-to-plan map\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** You are a guest at the Little Fulcrum Wellness Retreat, a spotless island run by a retired bathroom scale\nwho stopped weighing people and now measures only how much you wobble. Every activity (poses, reps, step class,\npaddling, a slackline, a tilting sand garden, sitting perfectly still while a frog judges you) is played through one\nverb: keep your centre of balance where it should be. Over five days the deadpan gets stranger. The plaques move.\nMarbles roll by themselves. The staff keep asking you to stand further east. Little Fulcrum is balanced on a single\nrock. On the last night the storm comes, the island tips, and in the finale your thumb's balance *is* the island's.\n\n**Genre:** a 3D sports and wellness anthology of balance activities around a small walkable island, with a daily-ritual\nmeta game and a comedy story. **Perspective:** third person throughout: a chase camera on the island, and a purpose-built\ncamera for each activity (the table in section 6). **Hub genre:** Sports.\n\n**Pillars**, for every decision:\n\n1. **One verb, many bodies.** The left stick is your weight. A new activity changes what your weight moves, never how\n   you move it. Fifteen seconds with any activity teaches the next one.\n2. **Your thumb is the exercise.** The character's limbs follow the input directly, by scrubbing animation (section 11).\n   Nothing plays \"a squat\" at you. You do the squat.\n3. **Deadpan kindness.** Nobody shouts, nothing is a game over. The game is polite, slightly invasive, and proud of you\n   in a way that worries you.\n4. **The daily ritual.** A morning check, one big number, a stamp, a growing stack of pebbles. Coming back is the game.\n5. **A small joke grows into the world.** The tilt starts as a sign that says \"Please keep to the centre\". It ends with\n   a storm and a whole island on your thumb.\n6. **A class, not a match.** Other people are on the next mat, wobbling. You can see them. It is enough.\n\n**Take from the source (feel and mechanics):** the centre-of-balance dot; holding still as a skill; lean-steered\nminigames; breathing; rhythm stepping; rep counting; a daily test that yields an \"age\"; a talking fitness peripheral\nwith personal questions; a serene instructor; earning time-based currency in a plaza; passive-aggressive encouragement.\n\n**Never take (this is an original game, not a recreation):** the word \"Wii\" anywhere in the game except the About\nscreen's Source line and the hub's source field; \"Fit Age\", \"Fit Credits\", \"Fit Piggy Bank\", \"Body Test\"; the source's\ntrainer look; the Balance Board's shape or lines; Mii-like avatars; Wuhu Island; the source's minigame names, course\nlayouts, menus, logo, fonts, jingles or melodies.\n\n**Never do, ever:** weight, BMI, calorie or body-size talk or numbers. All judgement is of *wobble*. That is also the\ngame's best running joke: the scale refuses to weigh anyone.\n\n---\n\n## 2. World and cast\n\n**Little Fulcrum**: a green and sand island about 200 m across, one low summit, a clean white retreat scattered on it,\nopen sea to every horizon. It looks like a brochure. Everything is very slightly too tidy.\n\nStation layout, in metres (x east, z south, island centre at 0,0; tune by eye, keep the relations):\n\n| Place | Around | Holds |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ferry Pier and Beach | (0, 100) | Arrival, the ferry; the south shore |\n| The Plaza | (0, 34), flat, y≈8 | Tare's Platform (Morning Check), Noticeboard, your Cairn |\n| Pose Pavilion | (-52, 18), flat | Pose Class, Group Class: four mats, white floor, pale curtains, open sides |\n| Still Pond | (-70, -12), a hollow | Still Pond |\n| The Annex | (52, 22), flat | Reps Hall and Step Class under one long open roof, a mirror wall |\n| Guest Huts | (64, 52) | Your hut: sleep ends the day |\n| South-West Lagoon | (-60, 85) | The Line (headland to rock), Paddle Glide launch |\n| Sand Terrace | (30, -48) | Sand Garden |\n| North Cliff | (0, -88), 14 m drop | Cliff Plunge |\n| Hoop Beach | (62, 84) | Hoop Patrol |\n| Lighthouse | (84, -60) | Landmark; the storm's beam |\n| East Terrace | (74, 8) | The \"high side\" in the finale |\n| Centre Stone | (0, 0), the summit | A plain stone with a brass plate reading \"Do not\". The finale ends here |\n\n**Cast** (all original, pronouns as given):\n\n- **Tare** (it): the mascot and guide. A squat round dial bathroom scale on two stubby legs, face in the dial. Retired\n  from weighing. Hops beside you. Chirpy, nosy, secretly sad. Keeps the secret of the rock badly.\n- **Instructor Plumb** (they): head instructor. Tall, narrow, utterly vertical. Pale mint tracksuit, eyes always closed\n  in two calm arcs, one very long straight plait that has never once moved. Speaks slowly and flatly. In the storm the\n  plait swings for the first time, and that is the most frightening moment in the game. At dawn Plumb opens their eyes,\n  once.\n- **Gerald** (he): a retired guest with immense calves, always already there, always finished. Says only \"Morning.\"\n  until Day 4.\n- **Bex** (they): a bored teenager sent here by a parent, holding a phone-shaped stone, annoyingly excellent.\n- **Old Nan** (she): 91, Wobble Age 19. Stands where you left her. Nobody has seen her arrive.\n- **The Gull** and **the Frog** (it): recurring menaces. The Gull lands on you at the worst moment. The Frog just looks.\n- **You**: \"Guest\". Pick one of three looks and a tracksuit colour at the pier. No name entry.\n\n**The five-day story** (delivered in one-liners, never in walls of text):\n\n| Day | Beat |\n|---|---|\n| 1 Arrival | Ferry leaves. Tare greets you. First Wobble Age is terrible. Sign: \"Please keep to the centre.\" |\n| 2 The Mat | Plaques have moved. \"Guests are reminded to distribute themselves evenly.\" |\n| 3 The Water | Drinks slide along tables. A notice calls this \"a feature of the drink\". |\n| 4 The Sand | Marbles in the Sand Garden roll with nobody there. Gerald speaks. At night Tare confesses: the island sits on one rock. \"We don't mention it to guests. You're a guest. I didn't mention it.\" |\n| 5 The Tilt | Group Class. Then the storm, The Great Tilt (section 7), dawn, the last Morning Check, credits. |\n| After | \"You may stay as long as you like. The ferry does not return.\" Free Play, and a fresh Morning Check every in-game day. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. The one verb: the Wobble Model\n\nThis is the game's core. Build it first, tune it in dev mode, and reuse it everywhere. It lives in the sim as a\ncomponent `wobble` on each balancing body, one per seat.\n\n**The centre of balance (CoB)** is a point in a unit disc: `x` left to right, `y` back to forward. `r = 1` is the edge:\npast it you topple.\n\nEach step (1/60 s), for each axis:\n\n```\ntarget  = slew(stick + mouse, 4 units/s) × (gentle ? 0.4 : 1) × reach      // clamped to the disc\naccel   = gain × (target − cob) + instability × cob + noise(t) × (1 − calmEffect × calm) + disturbance\nvel    += accel × dt;   vel *= exp(−damping × dt);   cob += vel × dt\n```\n\n| Setting | Default | Range | What it does |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `game.wobble.gain` | 14 /s² | 4–30 | How fast your weight follows your thumb |\n| `game.wobble.instability` | 3 /s² | 0–10 | How hard you fall away from the centre |\n| `game.wobble.damping` | 5 /s | 1–12 | How heavy the body feels |\n| `game.wobble.noise` | 3.5 /s² | 0–8 | Sway strength |\n| `game.wobble.noiseRate` | 0.15 Hz | 0.05–0.4 | Sway speed: a slow push you lean against |\n| `game.wobble.reach` | 0.9 | 0.5–1 | Full stick's target; a held full stick topples, on purpose |\n| `game.breath.period` | 8 s | 4–14 | One breath, half in, half out |\n| `game.breath.calmEffect` | 0.5 | 0–1 | How much full calm quiets the sway |\n\nSway per axis, phases and small rate ratios `m` drawn once per seat from a named stream (`w.rng('sway')`), never\n`Math.random`, and written with plain `Math.sin` so the sim's math swap reaches it:\n\n```\nnoise(t) = ( sin(2π·rate·m1·t + p1) + 0.6·sin(2π·rate·1.7·m2·t + p2) + 0.3·sin(2π·rate·3.1·m3·t + p3) ) / 1.3      // mi in 0.8–1.25\n```\n\n**These numbers are modelled, and the slow sway matters [tested in a small simulation of this exact model, 20 s, 10 seeds,\nstaying in a ring of 0.22]:** no input 38%; a human-like thumb (250 ms reaction, proportional gain 1.2, a little jitter) 90%;\na sloppy thumb (350 ms, gain 0.8) 72%; a no-delay bot 100%. A ring of 0.16 drops the thumb to 65%. Off-centre seats out to\n0.35 hold at 87%, and 0.6 starts to topple (so keep seats within 0.5). **At the 0.45 Hz sway first tried, doing nothing beat\nthe human thumb,** because a 250 ms reaction turns correction into amplification, so keep the sway slow. Nobody can play by\nhand here: the delayed-thumb bot (section 16) is how you judge feel.\n\nThe universal 4 units/s slew is what makes keyboard feel analog: a quick tap is a small nudge, a held key is a full lean.\nSticks barely notice it. Mouse adds its own target with a slow decay (section 4).\n\n**Topple.** `r > 1` for 6 steps: the body stumbles (`reactTo` flinch, a stumble clip, a creak), the activity decides the\nprice, and the CoB returns to centre over a second. It is never a game over.\n\n**Breath.** The third input is both feet held. A guide ring swells and shrinks with `breath.period`. Holding both feet\nfills `lungs`; letting go empties them. `sync` is a running match between your lungs and the guide. Good sync raises\n`calm` (0–1), which quiets the sway, warms the picture slightly, softens the sound, and pays a bonus. Breath is used in\nstill activities and is the charge mechanic in fast ones. Being out of sync has no penalty beyond losing the help.\n\n**Feet.** Left and right foot are the two triggers (analog) or shoulders. Used for steps, rhythm, paddling, jogging and\none-leg holds. Both together is breath, or a jump in Step Class. That is every input the activities need: **Lean, Left\nFoot, Right Foot, Both Feet.**\n\n**Scoring.** Every activity yields 0–1000 and a rank: 0 **Present**, 300 **Adequate**, 550 **Composed**, 750 **Serene**,\n900 **Unsettling**. Pebbles earned: 1 to 5 by rank.\n\n**No softlocks.** Every activity and every finale step ends by itself: each step has a time box, after which it moves on with\na remark (\"We will call that a start.\"). A player who puts the pad down reaches the results screen.\n\n**Balance targets the tests enforce** (section 16) on 10 seeds at default settings, in every activity: the perfect bot\nscores ≥ 750, the human-like delayed thumb ≥ 550, the sloppy thumb ≥ 300, no input at all ≤ 500 and below the human-like\nthumb. Random mashing never crashes.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Controls\n\nThe DualSense on a TV is the design target. Keyboard and mouse are native, not stand-ins.\n\n| Verb | DualSense | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lean (activities) | Left stick (D-pad too) | WASD or arrows; Shift is gentle | Move: pointer-locked movement leans |\n| Left foot / Right foot | L2 / R2 (L1 / R1 too) | Q / E | Left button / Right button |\n| Both feet (breathe, jump) | L2 + R2 | Space | Both buttons |\n| Pause | Options | Esc | |\n| Walk (island) | Left stick | WASD; Shift walks slowly | |\n| Look (island) | Right stick | | Move (pointer lock) |\n| Talk, use, enter | Cross | E or Enter | Left button at the thing in view |\n| Hop | Square | Space | |\n| Programme (today's list) | Triangle | Tab | |\n| Wave | Circle | C | |\n| Menus | D-pad or stick, Cross, Circle | Arrows, Enter or Space, Esc | Hover moves focus, click picks |\n\nActivity-specific mouse feel: in Sand Garden the pointer's place on the tray is the tilt; in Hoop Patrol you circle the\nmouse; Step Class is left and right click on the beat. On the island the thing at the centre of the screen gets a highlight\nand its prompt, and left click uses it.\n\n**Prompts** follow the input used last (`input.lastDevice`), switching the same frame. Pad: PlayStation glyphs (Cross,\nCircle, Square, Triangle, L1, R1, L2, R2, sticks) drawn as inline SVG. Settings has \"Button icons: PlayStation / Xbox\"\n(A B X Y, LB RB LT RT). Keyboard and mouse: key caps and a mouse icon with the button lit. Never rely on the engine's\n`input.prompt()` text for pads: it guesses the family from `Gamepad.id` and defaults to Xbox **[source]**.\nRead the action's codes, pick the ones for the device with `deviceOf`, and map each code to your glyph.\n\n**Action maps** (`defineActionMap`, switched live with `engine.input.use(id)`):\n\n```ts\n// actionMap:balance, every activity. [tested]: Space on both feet gives both held; values read move actions too.\ndefineActionMap('actionMap:balance', {\n  description: 'The lean, the two feet, and anything else, which still pond punishes.',\n  actions: {\n    forward: ['KeyW', 'ArrowUp', 'PadUp', 'PadLeftStickUp'],\n    back: ['KeyS', 'ArrowDown', 'PadDown', 'PadLeftStickDown'],\n    left: ['KeyA', 'ArrowLeft', 'PadLeft', 'PadLeftStickLeft'],\n    right: ['KeyD', 'ArrowRight', 'PadRight', 'PadLeftStickRight'],\n    footl: ['KeyQ', 'MouseLeft', 'PadLeftTrigger', 'PadLeftShoulder', 'Space'],\n    footr: ['KeyE', 'MouseRight', 'PadRightTrigger', 'PadRightShoulder', 'Space'],\n    gentle: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight'],\n    act: ['KeyF', 'Enter', 'PadSouth', 'PadEast', 'PadWest', 'PadNorth'],\n  },\n  values: { 'game:r': 'right', 'game:l': 'left', 'game:f': 'forward', 'game:b': 'back', 'game:fl': 'footl', 'game:fr': 'footr' },\n  lock: true,\n});\n```\n\nThe sim reads raw lean as `game:r − game:l`, `game:f − game:b` (a key absent is 0), not the camera-turned `move`.\nTriggers count as \"held\" from 0.5 but their `values` are analog below that.\n\n`actionMap:roam` is the same walk block plus `jump: ['Space', 'PadWest']`, `use: ['KeyE', 'Enter', 'MouseLeft', 'PadSouth']`,\n`walk: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight']`, `plan: ['Tab', 'PadNorth']`, `wave: ['KeyC', 'PadEast']`, `lock: true`.\n\nFront-end screens use the engine's system layer (`uiAccept`, `uiCancel`, `uiUp`…), which no map can rebind, with a map that\nhas `lock: false`, or a click on the canvas behind a menu captures the pointer **[source]**. Sand Garden uses a copy of the\nbalance map with `lock: false`, so the pointer stays visible: the page sends its place as `game:pointer`.\n\nInside activities, read only the map's actions, never `uiAccept`: Space is also breath. Skipping a demonstration is `act`.\nThe first time a mouse player needs the lean, show \"Click to lean with the mouse\": browsers grant pointer lock only in a click.\n\n**Mouse lean:** while pointer-locked in an activity, the page adds `pointermove`'s `movementX/Y` per frame and sends\nthem with `engine.input.virtual.axis('game:mouse', [dx, dy])`. The sim keeps `mouseTarget += delta × sensitivity`,\ndecaying with a 0.6 s time constant. Sensitivity lives in Settings.\n\n**Rumble:** a light `vibrationActuator.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on topple, on beats in Step Class, and growing with\n`r` near the edge; try/catch around it; a Settings toggle.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Structure\n\n**The Programme.** A five-day residency: a first full play takes about 45 minutes. Each day is a Morning Check plus three\nactivities, from a data table in `labs/game/scenes/data/days.ts`:\n\n| Day | Activities |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Morning Check (tutorial) · Pose Class A (Seated Nothing, Patient Chair, Heron) · Still Pond · a walk to find the Noticeboard |\n| 2 | Morning Check · Reps Hall · Sand Garden · Pose Class B (Archer, Comma, Napkin) |\n| 3 | Morning Check · Paddle Glide · The Line · Step Class |\n| 4 | Morning Check · Hoop Patrol · Cliff Plunge · Island Jog with Gerald |\n| 5 | Morning Check · Group Class (you and CPU guests) · Pose Class C (Bridge, Lighthouse) · The Great Tilt |\n| After | Final Morning Check, credits, then Free Play with every activity and daily checks |\n\nFinishing all of a day lets you sleep at your hut (walk there, or pick it from the Programme). Every activity can also be\nreplayed from the Noticeboard once met. Activities have no fail state: finishing at any rank counts.\n\n**Getting around.** You may walk everywhere, and the island rewards it, but nothing requires it: every Programme and\nNoticeboard item has \"Go\", which fades to the station. A controller player on a sofa never has to cross the island.\n\n**Wobble Age.** The Morning Check's result, from 18 to 82: `age = round(18 + 64 × (1 − s)^k)`, `k` is `game.age.k` (1.3).\nOne big number, a remark, a stamp on the calendar, and a line graph of at most seven points. Always compared with yesterday,\nin a tone of mild scientific surprise.\n\n**Pebbles and your Cairn.** Pebbles are the currency: 1 to 5 per activity by rank, plus 20 hidden around the island. You\nnever spend them. They stack, one by one, on a physical cairn in the Plaza, with a satisfying clack. The cairn leans\nslightly according to your last Wobble Age and stands true at 18. Thresholds unlock things, shown on the Noticeboard:\n10 Expert variants (a smaller ring, more sway), 20 and 30 tracksuit colours and a headband, 40 the \"Unsettling\" stamp set.\n\n**Exploration.** 20 pebbles, 12 \"Did you know?\" plaques, and each cast member has 3–5 new lines each day. Sit on a bench\nto watch the view with the Meditate clip. Wave at Gerald. Plaques visibly creep downhill across the days.\n\n**Tilt as story.** A value `island.tilt` starts at 0.4° on Day 1 and rises by 0.5° a day, so drinks slide, marbles roll,\nloose props drift and the sea sits fractionally wrong at the horizon. Only the Great Tilt gives it weight in play.\n\n**Saves** go in the page store (`createStore`, prefix unique to your game): day, done list, ages, pebbles, finds,\noutfits, bests, settings. The sim never reads them: the page hands the sim its starting state through a scene action\n(`game.begin`, section 14). Settings has Erase with an in-game confirmation, never `confirm()`.\n\n**Built to grow.** Days, poses, charts, trays, courses, lines, lures and settings are all data tables, one file each, so\nlater content is adding rows. Keep it that way: it is part of the brief.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Activities\n\nEvery activity is a module exposing the same shape: `{ id, title, station, camera, map, setup, step, teardown, score,\nbot, attract, short? }` in `labs/game/scenes/events/`. `step` emits sim events; the page turns them into text, sound and\nHUD. `bot` writes the same seat input a player does, and is used by tests, attract mode and dev auto-play. `short` is the\n45-second form for rounds (section 9).\n\n| Activity | Station | Camera | Verbs |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Morning Check | Plaza | Front, low, slow push-in | Lean, a foot |\n| Pose Class | Pavilion | Three-quarter, drifting orbit round your mat | Lean, foot or both |\n| Reps Hall | Annex | Side three-quarter, mirror behind | Lean scrubs the body |\n| Step Class | Annex | Behind and above, class in view | Feet, both, some lean |\n| Still Pond | Pond | Fixed portrait, front | Both, and nothing else |\n| The Line | Lagoon | Behind and above, dolly along the line | Feet, lean x |\n| Paddle Glide | Lagoon | Chase, low | Lean, feet |\n| Sand Garden | Terrace | Top-down, slight perspective | Lean, or pointer |\n| Cliff Plunge | North Cliff | Side dolly, falling follow, water-level splash | Both, lean |\n| Hoop Patrol | Hoop Beach | Front, wide | Lean in circles, or feet |\n| Island Jog | Whole coast | Chase (engine `cam3=chase`) | Stick, feet |\n\n### Morning Check (about 75 s)\n\nTare's Platform. Tare asks one yes/no question for the day (it changes only the remark). Then three tests:\n\n1. **Still Ring**, 12 s: stay inside a ring of r = 0.22, narrowing to 0.16 for the last 4 s. `A` = share of steps inside.\n2. **Reach**, up to 24 s: six targets (r = 0.16) at radius 0.5 in a seeded order; hold each 0.5 s. `B` = 1 − time/24.\n3. **One Foot**, 8 s: hold the foot Tare names; instability × 1.6; ring r = 0.3. Letting go pauses and drains. `C` = share inside.\n\n`s = 0.45A + 0.3B + 0.25C`; score `s × 1000`; Wobble Age as in section 5. Reveal: a large CSS dial spins past 100, \"recalibrating\",\novershoots, and settles. The Day 1 version introduces each verb with one line at a time.\n\n### Pose Class (about 2.5 min, three poses)\n\nPlumb demonstrates on the front mat (4 s, skippable). \"Now you.\"\n\n- **Enter:** a guide dot travels a curved path on the disc from centre to the pose's *seat*. Stay within 0.18 of it and\n  progress advances; progress scrubs the pose strip, so the body goes into the pose exactly as your thumb does. If you never\n  follow, Plumb waits 10 s and moves on: \"We will call that a start.\"\n- **Hold:** `game.pose.hold` s (12) with the CoB in the seat ring (`game.pose.ring`, 0.2), breathing with the guide, while\n  your off-seat lean visibly tips the body over. CPU guests hold too. Plumb does not move.\n- **Release:** \"And rest.\" Score: `1000 × (0.7 × inRing + 0.3 × sync) − 60 × topples`, class mean is the result.\n\n| Pose (id) | What it is | Seat (x, y) | Rule |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Seated Nothing (`nothing`) | Cross-legged, hands on knees | 0, 0 | Sway × 0.5; breath weighs 60% |\n| The Patient Chair (`chair`) | Knees bent, arms forward | 0, −0.35 | Hold both feet throughout, so no breath; sway climbs with time |\n| The Heron (`heron`) | One leg, foot on thigh, palms overhead | 0, 0 | Hold the standing foot; instability × 1.5 |\n| The Archer (`archer`) | Long lunge, arms out | 0.35, 0.35 | Instability × 1.2 |\n| The Sideways Comma (`comma`) | Side bend | 0.5, 0 then −0.5, 0 | Two holds, 8 s each |\n| The Folded Napkin (`napkin`) | Forward fold | 0, 0.5 | Low camera; the Gull lands on your back halfway |\n| The Polite Bridge (`bridge`) | On your back, hips lift | 0, 0 | Hips follow your lungs; breath weighs 70% |\n| The Lighthouse (`lighthouse`) | Tiptoe, arms straight up | 0, 0 | Ring 0.14; sway × 1.2 |\n\n### Reps Hall (about 90 s, three exercises)\n\nThe signature \"thumb is the exercise\" activity. A tall gauge on the HUD shows a band moving at the tempo\n(`game.reps.tempo`, 3 s a rep). Stick depth moves your marker and scrubs the body. Stay within 0.2 of the band to bank the\nrep. Gerald, beside you, does two for each of yours.\n\n- **Deep Knees** (10): pull back to squat, return to stand.\n- **Polite Push-Ups** (8): hold both feet (\"palms on the floor\") and scrub the push-up clip with the stick.\n- **Side Lunges** (10): stick x, alternating sides.\n\nThe band speeds up a little and sway rises near the end. Near the finish the counter cheats: \"9 of 10\" becomes \"10 of\n11\". Plumb: \"Four more. I may be lying.\" Score: mean rep accuracy.\n\n### Step Class (about 100 s)\n\nRhythm on a step platform in the Annex, three phrases at 104, 112 and 120 BPM (`game.step.bpm` scales them), charts as\nstrings in `labs/game/scenes/data/charts.ts`: `\"L . R . L . R . | L L R . B . . .\"`. Footprints scroll to a hit line on the\nHUD, floor tiles light in the world. Notes: L, R, B (both, a jump) and side steps (lean plus foot).\n\nWindows: Perfect ±3 steps, Good ±7 (`game.step.perfect`, `game.step.good`). Combo adds up to × 1.5. The guests behind you\nfollow your combo: lose it and they fall out of time, one by one, politely.\n\n**Beat audio goes on the effects bus, not the music bus.** Music defaults to 10%, so the song is only a bed. Sim `beat`\nevents fire claps and kicks at full level. The bed has no drums, so nothing flams against it. Timing is in sim steps,\nnever audio time.\n\n### Still Pond (75 s; 45 s in rounds)\n\nThe funniest thing in the game, and almost no input. You sit cross-legged on a jetty at dawn. **Composure** starts at 1000.\nThe only correct input is breathing with the guide. Anything else is \"You moved\": stick past 0.25, any button, a stray\nfoot, or mouse movement past a small threshold. Each costs `game.pond.penalty` (80), debounced 1 s, and sends a ripple\nacross the pond while the Frog turns to look.\n\nScore: `Composure × (0.5 + 0.5 × sync)`, so doing nothing at all earns \"Adequate\".\n\nLures, from a seeded table, every 6–10 s, escalating:\n\n- A big glyph prompt for whichever device you use: \"PRESS NOW\".\n- Tare: \"Psst. Is this on? Press ✕ for yes.\"\n- The phone-shaped stone buzzes.\n- The Frog sits on your knee. The Gull lands on your head.\n- A fake pause menu slides in with \"Resume\" highlighted.\n- \"Your controller appears to be disconnected. Press any button.\"\n- Plumb, very quietly: \"You are doing it wrong.\"\n- The credits roll. \"Thanks for playing! Press ✕ to continue.\"\n\nReal pause (Options, Esc) always works and costs nothing. Ending: \"You did nothing for seventy-five seconds. Nobody has\never been prouder of you. Nobody was watching.\"\n\n### The Line (about 75 s)\n\nA 40 m slackline across the lagoon with three flags. Alternate feet to step: each step moves 0.75 m and kicks you sideways\n(`game.line.stepKick`, 0.35); the same foot twice is a stumble. Lean x fights it. Gusts every 6–10 s, telegraphed 1.5 s ahead\nby ripples and a flag on Tare's little boat (`game.line.gust`). At 15 m the Gull lands on your arm, adding a steady bias\nuntil you flick it off with a fast stick flip. Falling is a large splash, then Tare's boat takes you back to the last\nflag: \"You have left the line. The line is fine.\" After 120 s the boat collects you: \"The line closes at this time.\"","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1060}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01RsHh8nsFEE12FiE3ABwBqj","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
