{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjxnJSf3qp8vTeS7frZZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game is built and handed in, but it isn't finished against the whole plan, and I couldn't get a clean browser run of the final build.\n\n**What I handed in** (both in `work/`):\n- `WellWobbled-WiiFit-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-031600.html`: one 6.9 MB file that opens from `file://` and fetches nothing. It has no audio files and the AI Hub client is byte-identical.\n- `exit-interview.json`, plus the dev folder `WellWobbled-WiiFit-claude-sonnet-5-5-medium-20261105-031600/`.\n\n**Checked on this machine**\n- **Node stages:** `npm run check` passes with the engine unchanged. The activity tests pass on 10 seeds against the balance targets, including random-input crash checks; I only ran the final tests on the earlier code, not after the last edits.\n- **Whole game:** the scripted playthrough finishes every day, Group Class and the Great Tilt on 3 seeds. The attract loop and capture/restore tests pass.\n- **AI Hub harness:** it passed at 2, 3 and 4 players and with the host leaving when each was run on its own. In the last full `verify` run, the 4-player case and an earlier host-leave run failed on \"no end within the grace period\". Each rerun on its own passed, so I'm treating those as flakes from machine load.\n\n**Not checked**\n- **Browser stage:** the browser checks (controller, keyboard, 10-foot text, attract, touch-only, About, no-WebGPU) passed 18 of 20 on an earlier build. I then fixed Escape closing a menu and opening pause at the same time, and re-ran on the final build. That run crashed (\"Target crashed\") on every attempt because the shared machine was short on memory. The last full `verify` (64/87) was dominated by load failures. So the final build has not had a clean browser pass.\n- **Screenshots:** I looked at the title, island and Pose Class screenshots. The Programme, pond, line, results, pause, Dev Mode and About screens were not checked by eye.\n\n**Not done from the plan**\n- Golden replay files were not recorded; only the same-seed and capture/restore tests cover reproducibility.\n- Expert variants, the extra tracksuit colours and headband, the Gull and Frog as critters, Plumb's swinging plait and closed-eye arcs, and a leaning cairn are missing.\n- Some art is rough: the pavilion floor is plain terrain.\n- `labs/game/ui/app.ts` is 595 lines, under the hard cap of 600 but over the 400 soft limit.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 03:16:00\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Sports: a 3D anthology of balance activities around a small walkable island, with a daily-ritual meta game and a comedy story. Third person throughout: a chase camera on the island, and a stage camera for each activity.\n- **Tech:** my-3dge (pinned at the commit in `engine-lock.json`), TypeScript, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21, Vite, Vitest, Playwright.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native toon puppets and generators, with new retreat assets: high-key brochure daylight, mint, sand and coral on clinical white.\n- **Animation:** the engine's clip layer and humanoid, with a new hand-written clip set (RETREAT) scrubbed from the sim by the guest's thumb.\n- **Audio:** all code: tracker-notation songs (music at 10% by default), effects, ambience loops and Animalese-style gibberish voices; no audio files.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, mode ghost (a seeded three-class Round, up to four players), plus same-screen Group Class on its own.\n- **Testing:** headless scripts only: Vitest in Node (the four stand-in players on every activity, a whole-game playthrough), Playwright with software-rendered Chromium for boot, controller, keyboard, attract, touch and hub-harness checks; screenshots only at the end.\n- **Research:** none beyond the plan; I read the engine guide and the AI Hub README once.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-sonnet-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **Issues caught:** 16. They include terrain rebuilt on every call (650 ms a step), a register array overrun that caused a data error, a custom input action silently overridden each frame, a missed button edge, and the hub card not answering Enter before the view existed. Others were `ready()` called too late under load, Still Pond scoring 748 instead of 500 when idle, five activities with bots or balance that needed fixing (The Line, Hoop Patrol, Cliff Plunge, Sand Garden, Great Tilt), Escape closing a menu and opening pause together, and the lint and file-length caps.\n- **Critical / game-breaking:** 7. They are the terrain rebuild, the register overrun, the overridden input map, the missed button edge, the hub card, the late `ready()`, and a Tilt bot that couldn't finish the finale.\n- **Most helpful test:** the four stand-in players (perfect, human-like, sloppy, idle) on every activity, plus the scripted whole-game playthrough.\n- **Least helpful test:** screenshots. Software-rendered WebGPU takes seconds a frame, so a shot taken before the first frame in a new area is solid black.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** everything is data (props, clips, songs, the sim as hashed plain numbers), which made scripted play and bots cheap.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong:** yes. `getTerrain` rebuilds the heightfield on every call, which turned a first draft into minutes per step until I cached it in the game. The engine also resets the input map every sample, so my `input.use()` calls did nothing until I passed an `actionMap` option.\n- **Most important thing built myself:** an activity framework where one module shape runs per seat, so the same code plays the island, Group Class and a hub Round.\n- **Hardest part:** the page cannot add a mesh, so everything drawn has to be a sim entity, prop, actor or HTML.\n- **Engine changes:** none.\n- **One change I'd make:** let a running page switch scene, so a hub session could share the island scene. More items are in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** the plan marks what was tested versus only read in source, which saved a lot of guessing.\n- **Confusing:** the sheer size of one run: a full game, a hub protocol, a shell and a report together. Some of the plan's own seed lines also break its \"two short sentences\" rule, and the hub client's SHA has moved on from the one it quotes.\n- **Hardest thing:** keeping every file under the size caps while the number of systems grew; prettier expanded one dense file to nearly three times its length.\n- **What I'd change:** a smaller first target, with the cut line stated as a milestone rather than a budget escape.\n\nThe design calls I made where the plan was silent or wrong are in `labs/game/README.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1198,"cache_read_input_tokens":651612,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1198},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e83c655a-6d0a-42b2-ac8e-79c6fd4e6b22","uuid":"0f2b2b42-3284-4659-9d3d-b2676939a1fb","timestamp":"2026-11-05T14:46:39.080Z","request_id":"req_011CgjxnJ93nFEfoX5sEBDzF"}
